// Source: to_cpp1.h (upstream's message for a local `_ := f();`).
// An object named `_` needs a declared type: `_ := f();` would keep the
// result alive, one character from `_ = f();`, which discards it. The rule
// applies at every scope, to `_: _` and `_: const _`, and to statement
// parameters; it is reported at the name.
// expect-error: (17,1): error: an object can have an anonymous name or an anonymous type, but not both at the same time (rationale: if '_ := f();' were allowed to keep the returned object alive
